["Bridging Language Gaps with Adaptive RAG: Improving Indonesian Language Question Answering 
II Related Works
II-D Single and Multi Step Retrieval
However recent studies around retrieval methods has been focused in multi-step retrieval, improving the ability of retrieval system to gather multiple information that is needed for answering specifically complex question [
[16](#bib.bib16)
] [
[17](#bib.bib17)
] . Studies have proposed methods such as SelfAsk [
[50](#bib.bib50)
] prompts an LLM to breakdown question into sub-questions so the prompt can get better by knowing what questions should be asked to get the final answer. Similarly DecomP or decomposed prompting [
[51](#bib.bib51)
] uses LLM to breakdown complex question into a more simpler task that is more manageable to complete. With the introduction of chain-of-thought prompting-where a model is encouraged to explain its reasoning process step by step rather than providing an answer directly-the reasoning performance of LLMs has improved significantly [
[52](#bib.bib52)
] . Building on this idea, several approaches have been proposed to enhance retrieval and reasoning. For example, ReAct [
[53](#bib.bib53)
] encourages LLMs to interleave reasoning with actions in their outputs, although this method tends to work more effectively with larger or fine-tuned models rather than smaller ones. More recently, Adaptive-RAG [
[17](#bib.bib17)
] integrates the state-of-the-art IRCoT [
[16](#bib.bib16)
] for multi-step retrieval, where an interleaving approach guides the retrieval process so that prompts can more effectively provide the context needed for answering questions comprehensively.", "Agent-Orchestrated Adaptive RAG: A Comparative  Study on Structured and Multi-Hop Retrieval
II Background and Related Work
II-D Reasoning and Multi-Hop Retrieval
Several prompting frameworks elicit explicit reasoning in LLMs. Chain-of-Thought prompting [
[48](#bib.bib54)
] encourages the model to generate intermediate reasoning steps before committing to an answer, improving performance on tasks that require composition. Least-to-Most prompting [
[54](#bib.bib50)
] decomposes a hard problem into a sequence of simpler sub-problems solved in order, an approach that aligns closely with the query decomposition strategy adopted in this work. Self-refinement methods iterate on a model's own draft using its self-generated feedback [
[28](#bib.bib52)
] , and inference-time self-correction has been surveyed as a general mechanism for improving reliability [
[20](#bib.bib18)
] .
Multi-hop question answering benchmarks provide the testbed for these methods. HotpotQA [
[52](#bib.bib6)
] popularized explainable multi-hop evaluation, and MuSiQue [
[43](#bib.bib7)
] was deliberately constructed by composing single-hop questions so that answering requires genuine evidence integration across independent documents and resists single-hop shortcuts. Tree- and graph-structured retrieval has been proposed to better capture the dependencies among reasoning steps [
[41](#bib.bib14)
] . Despite this activity, comparatively few studies place a multi-hop benchmark alongside a structured, domain-specific corpus to isolate how the same agentic mechanisms behave under different reasoning demands-the contrast this work is designed to expose."]

["RAG-Enhanced Large Language Models for Dynamic Content Expiration Prediction in Web Search CCS: Information systems Retrieval models and ranking CCS: Computing methodologies Natural language processing
4. Conclusion
In this paper, we propose a novel LLM-based Dynamic Content Expiration framework that shifts the paradigm of timeliness ranking in Baidu Search. Moving beyond rigid rules, our approach extracts fine-grained temporal contexts and applies dual-stage consistency verification to deduce reliable, query-specific validity horizons. By distilling these semantic judgments into lightweight discrete signals f exp f_{\text{exp}} , we successfully bridge complex temporal reasoning with the strict latency constraints of a production ranker. Extensive offline evaluations and online A/B test validate the framework's profound impact on holistic search quality.", "DailyQA: A Benchmark to Evaluate Web Retrieval Augmented LLMs Based on Capturing Real-World Changes
Introduction
Large language models (LLMs) has demonstrated its wide range of capabilities in the natural language processing (NLP) domain (
[Devlin 2018](#bib.bib5)
;
[Brown et al. 2020](#bib.bib2)
) and is extending its influence to more and more domains (
[Radford et al. 2021](#bib.bib18)
;
[Ramesh et al. 2021](#bib.bib19)
;
[Luo et al. 2022](#bib.bib15)
;
[Singhal et al. 2025](#bib.bib22)
;
[Salinas et al. 2020](#bib.bib21)
) . However, the world is changing fastly, and the static knowledge in the memory of LLMs is usually not updated in a timely manner (
[Dhingra et al. 2021](#bib.bib6)
) . A popular approach to this issue is to use retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) (
[Lewis et al. 2020](#bib.bib14)
) techniques to provide the language model with external knowledge, allowing the model to solve problems using in-text learning methods. However, this approach often relies on external retrievers based on keyword or semantic matching (
[Robertson and Zaragoza 2009](#bib.bib20)
;
[Karpukhin et al. 2020](#bib.bib11)
;
[Khattab and Zaharia 2020](#bib.bib13)
;
[Chatterjee, Mackie, and Dalton 2024](#bib.bib3)
;
[Guo et al. 2024](#bib.bib9)
;
[McDonald, Brokos, and Androutsopoulos 2018](#bib.bib16)
) . For time-sensitive queries, highly ranked documents may contain misleading information because they do not fulfill the time constraints, thus limiting the capabilities of the RAG system. So we design a time-sensitive query dataset based on realistic changes to measure the model's ability to adapt to rapidly changing information under time constraints."]

["Faithfulness-QA: A Counterfactual Entity Substitution Dataset for Training Context-Faithful RAG Models
2 Related Work
Faithfulness and Hallucination.
Faithfulness-the degree to which a model's output is supported by its input context-has been extensively studied in summarization (
[Maynez et al. 2020](#bib.bib18)
;
[Laban et al. 2022](#bib.bib11)
) and question answering (
[Li et al. 2022](#bib.bib14)
) . In the RAG setting, Self-RAG (
[Asai et al. 2024](#bib.bib1)
) introduces reflection tokens for self-critique, and CRAG (
[Yan et al. 2024](#bib.bib28)
) proposes corrective retrieval strategies. FaithfulRAG (
[Zhang et al. 2025a](#bib.bib29)
) decomposes knowledge conflicts at the fact level, while Drift (
[Li et al. 2025](#bib.bib16)
) uses dual-reward probabilistic inference to enhance rationale faithfulness. HaluEval (
[Li et al. 2023](#bib.bib13)
) and FaithEval (
[Ming et al. 2025](#bib.bib20)
) provide evaluation benchmarks for hallucination detection and context faithfulness, but neither is designed as a large-scale training resource.", "Faithfulness-QA: A Counterfactual Entity Substitution Dataset for Training Context-Faithful RAG Models
1 Introduction
Several lines of work have attempted to address this problem. Self-RAG (
[Asai et al. 2024](#bib.bib1)
) trains models to generate reflection tokens that critique their own outputs, but still relies on the model's internal judgment. FaithfulRAG (
[Zhang et al. 2025a](#bib.bib29)
) models knowledge conflicts at the fact level through a self-thinking process, yet focuses on inference-time conflict resolution rather than systematic training data construction. CoCoLex (
[Santosh et al. 2025](#bib.bib22)
) forces copy-based decoding at inference time, which is inherently limited to extractive answers. FaithEval (
[Ming et al. 2025](#bib.bib20)
) offers a benchmark with counterfactual, inconsistent, and unanswerable contexts, but is designed for
*evaluation*
(4,900 samples) rather than large-scale
*training*
.
A complementary approach is to construct
*training data*
that explicitly and systematically creates knowledge conflicts, so that a model trained on such data must learn to follow the context. The CounterFact dataset (
[Meng et al. 2022](#bib.bib19)
) demonstrated the value of counterfactual entity manipulation, but its purpose is
*knowledge editing*
rather than RAG faithfulness training. More recent work on knowledge conflicts in LLMs (
[Xu et al. 2024](#bib.bib27)
) has highlighted the urgent need for large-scale, controlled conflict datasets, yet no such resource targeted at RAG training currently exists.", "Faithfulness-QA: A Counterfactual Entity Substitution Dataset for Training Context-Faithful RAG Models
1 Introduction
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has become the dominant paradigm for grounding large language models (LLMs) in external evidence (
[Lewis et al. 2020](#bib.bib12)
;
[Guu et al. 2020](#bib.bib5)
) . By prepending retrieved documents to the input, RAG systems can, in principle, produce responses that faithfully reflect the most up-to-date or domain-specific information. In practice, however, models frequently
*ignore*
the retrieved context and instead fall back on parametric knowledge acquired during pre-training, a failure mode broadly categorized as
*unfaithfulness*
(
[Longpre et al. 2021](#bib.bib17)
;
[Xie et al. 2024](#bib.bib26)
) .
The root cause is structural: the standard next-token prediction loss treats every token identically, regardless of whether it originates from the context or from memorized parameters. Without explicit training signal that rewards context-grounding, there is no mechanism to steer the model's attention toward the retrieved passage when a conflict exists between external evidence and internal belief (
[Li et al. 2022](#bib.bib14)
) ."]

["Open Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Factual Image Generation
2 Related Work
Advances in Image Generation. Image generation has progressed from GAN-based models
[Zhang et al. 2017](#bib.bib37)
to diffusion models
[Rombach et al. 2022](#bib.bib27)
;
[Ramesh et al. 2022](#bib.bib25)
;
[Saharia et al. 2022](#bib.bib28)
, which significantly improved fidelity, diversity, and controllability. Recent developments have shifted toward LLM-based architectures, which can be broadly categorized into two primary paradigms: auto-regressive models that generate images from unified visual-text tokens
[Wang et al. 2024](#bib.bib30)
;
[Batifol et al. 2025](#bib.bib2)
, and hybrid models that use an LLM backbone to steer a diffusion decoder
[Zhou et al. 2024](#bib.bib39)
;
[Wu et al. 2025](#bib.bib32)
;
[Geng et al. 2025](#bib.bib7)
. However, despite their impressive realism, these models often suffer from factual inconsistency. This issue primarily stems from a fundamental gap between the models' static, internal knowledge and the dynamic, real-world facts
[Kalai and Vempala 2024](#bib.bib13)
;
[Xu et al. 2025](#bib.bib34)
. This limitation undermines their reliability in critical domains such as education, media, and science, where accuracy is crucial
[Robertson 2024](#bib.bib26)
;
[Liu et al. 2023](#bib.bib21)
.", "Re-Imagen: Retrieval-Augmented  Text-to-Image Generator
2 Related Work
Text-to-Image Diffusion Models There has been a wide-spread success (
[Ashual et al. 2022](#bib.bib3)
;
[Ramesh et al. 2022](#bib.bib26)
;
[Saharia et al. 2022](#bib.bib32)
;
[Nichol et al. 2021](#bib.bib22)
) in modeling text-to-image generation with diffusion models, which has outperformed GANs (
[Goodfellow et al. 2014](#bib.bib9)
) and auto-regressive Transformers (
[Ramesh et al. 2021](#bib.bib25)
) in photorealism and diversity (under similar model size), without training instability and mode collapsing issues. Among them, some recent large text-to-image models such as Imagen (
[Saharia et al. 2022](#bib.bib32)
) , GLIDE (
[Nichol et al. 2021](#bib.bib22)
) , and DALL-E2 (
[Ramesh et al. 2022](#bib.bib26)
) have demonstrated excellent generation from complex prompt inputs. These models achieve highly fine-grained control over the generated images with text inputs. However, they do not perform explicit grounding over external visual knowledge and are restricted to memorizing the visual appearance of every possible visual entity in their parameters. This makes it difficult for them to generalize to rare or even unseen entities. In contrast, Re-Imagen is designed to free the diffusion model from memorizing, as models are encouraged to retrieve semantic neighbors from the knowledge base and use retrievals as context to paint the image. Re-Imagen improves the grounding of the diffusion models to real-world knowledge and is therefore capable of faithful image synthesis."]
